<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="prep-3-17">
  <title>Resources for 3/17</title>

  <objectives>
    <introduction>
      <p>For the content of this class, you will be expected to be able to do the following:</p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain what an <em>adjacency matrix</em> of a graph is.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Given a graph, construct an adjacency matrix.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Given an adjacency matrix, construct the graph it represents.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Determine whether or not a given matrix is an adjacency matrix for a graph.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <subsection xml:id="video-activity-adjacency-matrix">
    <title>Video &amp; Activities for Adjacency Matrices</title>
    <p>
      Below is a YouTube video on <em>adjacency matrices</em>, which store the essential information of a graph. It is followed by a link to a set of activities that we will be working on in class. Please watch the video and do as many of the activities as you can before class.
    </p>

    <video youtube="IZzOgiYB4o4" />

    <problem>
      <title>Adjacency Matrices</title>
      <p>
        Work the following adjacency matrix problems.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Make two different adjacency matrices for the following graph.
            </p>
            <image width="60%">
              <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="image-prep-adjacency-1">
                <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
                  <definition>undirected1={1:[2,4,5],2:[5,6],3:[4,5],4:[6],5:[6]}</definition>
                  <definition>N=6</definition>
                  <definition>f(t)=(cos(2*pi*t/N), sin(2*pi*t/N))</definition>
                  <coordinates bbox="(-1.07,-1,1.07,1)">
                    <network directed="no" graph="undirected1" labels="yes" 
                    scale="0.8" node-stroke="black" node-fill="white" >
                      <node at="1" p="f(1)" />
                      <node at="2" p="f(2)" />
                      <node at="3" p="f(3)" />
                      <node at="4" p="f(4)" />
                      <node at="5" p="f(5)" />
                      <node at="6" p="f(6)" />
                    </network>
                  </coordinates>
                </diagram>
              </prefigure>
            </image>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Draw a graph for the following adjacency matrix.
              <md>
                \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}
              </md>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Explain why the following matrix is not an adjacency matrix.
              <md>
                \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}
              </md>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </problem>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Optional Reading</title>
    <p>
      <url href="">Adjacency Matrices for Graphs</url>
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>